# Issue #830, execution twin of `heritage/check/heritage-value-of-key-reader-in-parentheses`.
# `execute()` runs `parse()` and never `check()`, so the runtime keeps its own copy of these
# rules and the parenthesized reader has to be proven to RUN, not merely to parse clean.
#
# Every `print` below pins a VALUE that only exists if the `(` path actually entered the
# `value-of-reader` (`spec/grammar.md:203`, defined :217) instead of taking `value` as a
# `parenthesized-call` callee (:215). The reader operates on a dict, so Heritage also needs
# Data (`spec/conformance.md:305`).
:d = { a: 7 b: "a" }
:nested = { inner: { x: 9 } }

# The shape the issue reports: the whole reader wrapped in parentheses.
print (value of :d for key "a")

# A parenthesized reader as the reader's own DICTIONARY operand.
print value of (value of :nested for key "inner") for key "x"

# A parenthesized reader as the reader's own KEY operand: the inner reader reports the word
# "a", which the outer reader then uses as its key.
print value of :d for key (value of :d for key "b")

# Redundant grouping must not change the value.
print ((value of :d for key "a"))

# A parenthesized reader composes with the rest of the expression grammar.
print (value of :d for key "a") + 1
print 1 + (value of :d for key "a")

# A parenthesized reader takes postfixes, so it is a real `primary` (`spec/grammar.md:192`).
print (value of :nested for key "inner").x
print (value of :nested for key "inner")[x]

# Both readers parenthesized.
print (value of (value of :nested for key "inner") for key "x")

# The parenthesized reader agrees with the Core twins it is specified to correspond to.
print :d["a"]
print :nested.inner.x
